::MCR_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q01
MLA Standard: Unit 2 standards synthesis
Question:
A plan costs $25 plus $8 per visit. Which model gives total cost C for v visits?
{
~A. C = 25v + 8#This switches the fixed fee and per-visit rate.
=B. C = 8v + 25#Correct. The fixed cost is 25 and each visit adds 8.
~C. C = 33v#This treats both amounts as repeated per visit.
~D. C = 25 - 8v#This subtracts the visit cost instead of adding it.
}
::MCR_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q02
MLA Standard: Unit 2 standards synthesis
Question:
Solve 8v + 25 = 65.
{
~A. v = 4#This gives 57, which is too low.
=B. v = 5#Correct. Subtract 25 to get 40, then divide by 8.
~C. v = 8#This uses the rate as the number of visits.
~D. v = 11#This adds unrelated values.
}
::MCR_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q03
MLA Standard: Unit 2 standards synthesis
Question:
Which model represents 10% growth from an initial value of 300?
{
~A. A = 300(0.10)^t#This uses the percent as the factor instead of 1.10.
~B. A = 300 + 10t#This is linear, not exponential growth.
=C. A = 300(1.10)^t#Correct. Ten percent growth uses factor 1.10.
~D. A = 1.10(300)^t#This places the initial value in the exponent position.
}
::MCR_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q04
MLA Standard: Unit 2 standards synthesis
Question:
A fee is $6 up to 2 pounds and $10 over 2 pounds. What is the cost for exactly 2 pounds?
{
=A. $6#Correct. Up to 2 pounds includes exactly 2 pounds.
~B. $10#This is for weights over 2 pounds.
~C. $16#This adds both rules instead of choosing the interval.
~D. $4#This subtracts values and does not match the rule.
}
::MCR_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U02_L08_GuidedPractice_Q05
MLA Standard: Unit 2 standards synthesis
Question:
What does a break-even point mean?
{
~A. One option has no cost#Break-even does not mean free.
~B. The slopes are always equal#Slopes can be different at break-even.
~C. The y-intercepts are always equal#Y-intercepts can be different.
=D. Two models have equal values at the same input#Correct. Break-even means the totals match at the same input.
}